Rejects a terrain exaggeration outside the style spec's range. The terrain
converter stored any number, so a style document could set a negative
exaggeration, or one such as 1e39 that overflows the float to infinity, and
the map drew and reported heights from it. The converter now fails for a
value that is negative or not finite, so a style document loads without its
terrain and logs why, as for any other terrain it cannot convert.
Upstream: not proposed

diff --git a/src/mln/style/conversion/terrain.cpp b/src/mln/style/conversion/terrain.cpp
index a447d9ba95..b203d3c257 100644
--- a/src/mln/style/conversion/terrain.cpp
+++ b/src/mln/style/conversion/terrain.cpp
@@ -2,6 +2,8 @@
 #include <mln/style/conversion/constant.hpp>
 #include <mln/style/conversion_impl.hpp>
 
+#include <cmath>
+
 namespace mln {
 namespace style {
 namespace conversion {
@@ -34,6 +36,11 @@ std::optional<Terrain> Converter<Terrain>::operator()(const Convertible& value,
             error.message = "terrain exaggeration must be a number";
             return std::nullopt;
         }
+        // The style spec's minimum is 0. A float overflows to infinity past its range.
+        if (!std::isfinite(*converted) || *converted < 0.0f) {
+            error.message = "terrain exaggeration must be a finite number of at least 0";
+            return std::nullopt;
+        }
         exaggeration = *converted;
     }
 
